Day 9 – String Concepts in Java

Full Stack Web Development with Java Internship

Today’s session focused on understanding the concept of Strings in Java and how they are used to handle textual data in programs.

🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ Introduction to Strings

A String is a sequence of characters used to store text.

Strings in Java are objects of the String class.

Strings are immutable (cannot be changed once created).

2️⃣ Creating Strings

Two ways to create a String:

Using String Literal

String name = "Java";

Using new Keyword

String name = new String("Java");
3️⃣ Important String Methods

The mentor explained commonly used built-in methods:

length() → Returns the length of the string

charAt(index) → Returns character at specified index

equals() → Compares two strings

substring() → Extracts a part of the string

toUpperCase() → Converts string to uppercase

These methods help in manipulating and processing textual data efficiently.

4️⃣ String Pool and Memory Management

Strings created using literals are stored in the String Pool.

The String Pool avoids duplicate objects to save memory.

Strings created using new keyword are stored in heap memory.

If two literals have the same value, they refer to the same memory location.

🎯 Learning Outcomes

Understood how strings are created and stored in memory.

Learned important built-in string methods.

Gained clarity on immutability of strings.

Understood the concept of the String Pool and memory optimization.